 Only possibility is horizontal left: A6 is *, so A7 would sandwich nothing because only A6 and A7 — no @ between. Wait: A6 is *, A7 is new *, so no @ between → no flip. But wait — condition requires that between the new piece and an existing * in some direction, all are @. Here, from A7 to A6 (*): no @ in between → invalid for flipping. But is (A,7) even valid? Our list says it's valid, so must be another direction. Check diagonally: A7 to B6? B6 is @, C5 is *, so path: A7(new*), B6(@), C5(*) — yes! That diagonal (southeast) would flip B6. So only one piece flipped. Minimal gain.\n- (A,8): Corner move. A8 is adjacent to A7 (.), B7 (@), B8 (*). If we play A8, then check connections: down the file: A7 is . → no vertical. Diagonally: B7 is @, and B8 is * → so A8(new*) - B7(@) - B8(*) → yes, flips B7. That’s one piece. Horizontal: A7 is . → nothing. So only one flip. But corner value is high — though less than A1 because A8 is less stable? Actually both corners are equally safe once taken. But A1 offers much more immediate flip payoff.\n- (C,1): C1 is on the bottom-left edge. Currently, B1 is *, D1 is *, so vertically: B1(*), C1(empty), D1(*) — placing * at C1 would sandwich nothing because no @ between. Horizontally: C1 to C2: C2 is @, C3 is @, C4 is @, C5 is *, so placing at C1 would allow a sandwich: C1(*) - C2(@), C3(@), C4(@) - C5(*) → flips C2, C3, C4 — 3 pieces. Also, diagonally: D2 is @, E3 is @, F4 is @, G5 is *, so C1-D2-E3-F4-G5: all @ in between? C1(new*), D2(@), E3(@), F4(@), G5(*) → yes, flips D2, E3, F4 — 3 more. Total 6 pieces flipped! This is a very strong move.\n- (H,8): Corner. Adjacent to G8(*), H7(@). Placing * at H8: up the file: H7 is @, H6 is @, G8 is *, but diagonal? G7 is @, G8 is *, so H8(new*) - G7(@) - G8(*) → flips G7. Vertically: H7(@), H6(@), but H5 is *, so H8-H7-H6-H5: H8(new*), H7(@), H6(@), H5(*) → flips H7 and H6 — 2 pieces. Diagonal gives one, vertical gives two — total 3 pieces flipped. Also secures the H8 corner — valuable.\n\nReason: While (A,1) offers a huge flip of 5 pieces and secures a corner, (C,1) is even better — it flips 6 pieces in two directions (vertical and diagonal), significantly increasing my control in the center and left side. It also helps solidify the left flank and connects well with existing pieces. Though not a corner itself, C1 supports dominance along the first column and fifth row. The sheer number of flips makes this the most impactful move. (A,1) flips 5 (A2–A6), but (C,1) flips 3 horizontally (C2–C4) and 3 diagonally (D2, E3, F4), totaling 6. Therefore, (C,1) maximizes immediate material gain and board control.\n\nAnswer: (C,1)"}
